e2e-encryption = End-to-End Encryption
e2e-desc = Pastes are encrypted in your browser before upload. Your data is encrypted with a random per-paste content key (CEK), which is itself wrapped with a key derived from your password — so the password never directly touches your content and can be changed without re-encrypting. Keys never leave your device.
e2e-item1 = Argon2id(password, salt) → 32-byte master key (19456 KiB memory, 2 iterations)
e2e-item2 = HKDF-SHA256(master, "mitsuzo-encryption-key") → 32-byte key-encryption key (KEK)
e2e-item3 = HKDF-SHA256(master, "mitsuzo-validation-key") → 32-byte validation key
e2e-item4 = Random 32-byte content key (CEK) per paste, wrapped with ChaCha20-Poly1305(KEK)
e2e-item5 = ChaCha20-Poly1305(CEK, derived_nonce, plaintext) → ciphertext; files >64KB split into 64KB chunks with unique derived nonces
e2e-item6 = Unique 16-byte salt, 12-byte base nonce and 12-byte wrap nonce per paste
e2e-item7 = Server never sees your password, plaintext, or unwrapped CEK
zk-validation = Zero-Knowledge Password Validation
zk-desc = The server validates your password without ever knowing it. Here's how:
zk-step1 = Argon2id + HKDF derive the KEK and validation key from your password
zk-step2 = Browser computes HMAC-SHA256(validation_key, salt) and sends this hash to the server
zk-step3 = Server stores only this hash — never the key, salt, or password
zk-step4 = When viewing, browser re-derives keys and hash, sending it in the X-Password-Hash header
zk-step5 = Server compares hashes in constant time (XOR-based) — a match proves the password is correct without revealing anything
zk-note = A compromised server should not decrypt pastes or recover passwords; weak passwords and compromised clients remain outside this guarantee.
workflow-diagram = Workflow Diagram
workflow-legend = The diagram shows three flows: password-sealed pastes, pastes encrypted to a user account, and account management.
workflow-legend-password = Password mode: the content key is wrapped with a key derived from the paste password; the server only ever sees an HMAC of the validation key.
workflow-legend-recipient = Recipient mode: the content key is sealed to the recipient's X25519 public key via ECDH — both the recipient and the sender (who keeps the ephemeral key) can open it.
workflow-legend-accounts = Accounts are zero-knowledge X25519 identities derived from a BIP39 seed phrase; authentication uses single-use challenges solved with ECDH.
self-destructing = Self-Destructing Pastes
self-destruct-desc = You control how long your paste lives. Set a time-to-live (TTL) and optionally a maximum number of views.
sd-item1 = Automatic deletion after TTL expires (max 12 hours)
sd-item2 = Optional try-count limit for extra security (1-100 attempts)
sd-item3 = Paste permanently deleted when try-count reaches 0
sd-item4 = Failed decryption attempts count toward try-count
burn-after-title = Burn After Reading
burn-after-desc = When enabled, the paste is automatically deleted after the first successful decryption. This is enforced by a cryptographic receipt — the client proves it fully decrypted the content before the server deletes it.
ba-item1 = Paste deleted immediately after first successful view
ba-item2 = Cryptographic burn receipt proves decryption, no client trust required
ba-item3 = Falls back to TTL expiry if the client never sends the receipt
ba-item4 = Failed attempts still count toward the try-count limit
proof-safety = Proof of Safety
proof-safety-desc = End-to-end encryption is designed to protect plaintext from the storage server when the client and password are trustworthy. The server stores encrypted chunks, a wrapped content key, and a validation proof; it still sees operational metadata needed to serve and expire pastes.
ps-item1 = A storage-server compromise should not reveal plaintext without the password or client-held key
ps-item2 = Decryption keys are intended to remain in your browser or CLI
ps-item3 = Self-destructing pastes minimize exposure window
ps-item4 = Failed attempts can be capped to prevent brute-force attacks
ps-item5 = Password changes never re-upload or re-encrypt your data
cli-usage = CLI Client
cli-desc = Mitsuzo also provides a cross-platform CLI client for creating and retrieving pastes from the terminal.
cli-create = Create: cli create [-f file] [-c tries] [-t ttl] [-b]
cli-get = Get: cli get <id> [-o output]
cli-passwd = Change password: cli passwd <id>
cli-account = Account: mitsuzo account register | account login | account import
docs-accounts-title = Accounts & Paste-to-User
docs-accounts-desc = Mitsuzo accounts are zero-knowledge identities. An account is a BIP39 seed phrase that deterministically derives an X25519 key pair. The server stores only the public key and a display name; your private key is encrypted with an account password and kept on your device.
docs-accounts-item1 = Your seed phrase is the only backup — anyone with it controls your account. Write it down.
docs-accounts-item2 = Login is challenge-based: the server sends a challenge sealed to your public key, you prove possession of the key with ECDH, and nothing is stored server-side.
docs-accounts-item3 = Paste-to-user: from a profile page click "Send encrypted message" — the content key is sealed to the recipient's public key via X25519 ECDH.
docs-accounts-item4 = By ECDH symmetry the same envelope opens for the recipient (via their account key) and the sender (via the ephemeral key) — so the sender can reopen their own sent paste.
docs-accounts-item5 = Fetching a paste encrypted to an account requires a solved X-Account-Proof challenge instead of a password.
